<resources>
    <!-- The original Android Studio template's own placeholder swatch
         (purple_200/500/700, teal_200/700) lived here unreferenced by
         anything — confirmed by grepping the whole project — until
         removed investigating a "system status/nav bar is some dark blue"
         report: `Theme.vault` (`themes.xml`) never mapped any of
         them to `colorPrimary`/`colorPrimaryDark`, so Android was actually
         falling back to Material Components' own built-in default indigo/
         purple palette wherever the theme didn't win outright — the SAME
         color family as this leftover swatch, and a very plausible source
         of exactly that report, even though nothing here was technically
         "in use." See `MainActivity.kt`'s own doc on the actual fix. -->
    <color name="black">#FF000000</color>
    <color name="white">#FFFFFFFF</color>

    <!-- The Figma home-screen design's own root-frame background
         (node 103:175, `HomeScreen` — `#292c2f`, a flat fill as of the
         design's latest revision; an earlier revision had this as a
         `#3a4148`→`#2a3036` gradient, which is why `VaultScreen.tsx`'s own
         matching web-side value is a plain literal there too, not a named
         token — it already went through one revision and a second literal
         swap costs nothing extra). Used as this native theme's own
         `windowBackground` below, and directly in `MainActivity.kt` for
         the system status/navigation bar color, so all three — the web
         content's own "Outside" plane, the native window background behind
         it, and the system bars around it — are always the exact same
         value by construction, not three independently-chosen ones that
         can drift out of sync.
         `Theme.vault`'s parent (`Theme.MaterialComponents.DayNight.
         NoActionBar`) had no background override at all until this was
         added, so the native window fell back to whatever the platform's
         own day/night default resolves to (a light background in day mode,
         a generic Material dark grey in night mode) — neither of which is
         this app's actual color. That default is what's briefly visible on
         cold start before the WebView paints, and in any edge-to-edge
         inset area the WebView doesn't cover (`enableEdgeToEdge()` in
         `MainActivity.kt`). This color previously matched a DIFFERENT app
         token (`ink-950`, `#070e19`, the older ink-design-system's own page
         background) as a reasonable stand-in chosen before the Figma
         design specified an exact value for this particular surface — now
         that it has, this reflects that instead; `ink-950` itself is
         unrelated and still exactly where it always was in
         `tailwind.config.js`, for the screens that still use it
         (Settings, the entry editor). -->
    <color name="home_background">#FF292C2F</color>
</resources>
